MYTHS = [
    ("hardware-penalty-is-universal", "A branch misprediction has the same cycle cost on every processor", "Recovery depends on the selected pipeline, state and overlap. A fixed penalty is used here only inside an explicit additive model; the Intel manual supplies selected mechanisms, not one universal timing constant.", "recovery-work"),
    ("every-cache-miss-reaches-dram", "Every level one cache miss transfers a full line from DRAM", "A lower cache can satisfy that miss. DRAM traffic must be counted at the DRAM boundary with the specified downstream hits, coalescing and other transfers; level-one misses alone do not determine it.", "memory-traffic"),
    ("more-ways-always-fewer-misses", "More associativity at fixed capacity can never increase cache misses", "Replacement behavior changes too. The declared two-line read-allocating trace [0,1,3,0] has three direct-mapped misses but four fully associative LRU misses. That does not contradict increasing capacity within a fixed fully associative LRU stack model.", "associativity"),
    ("branch-bias-fixes-misses", "Branch outcomes determine the mismatch count without a starting predictor state", "The declared counter model depends on its starting state and update rule. Identical alternating taken/not-taken outcomes produce different mismatch counts from initial states zero and one.", "cold-predictor"),
    ("branch-sites-train-independently", "Different branch sites always have independent prediction histories", "Distinct sites can map to a shared predictor entry and train shared state. The tagless example demonstrates this possibility without claiming that every Intel predictor uses its indexing function.", "predictor-alias"),
    ("simd-width-is-speedup", "Eight SIMD lanes guarantee eight times lower elapsed time", "Lane width specifies grouping, not achieved speedup. Executed groups, tails, dependencies, operation mix and memory service all matter; a higher compute ceiling does not establish the measured result.", "vector-width"),
    ("branchless-is-always-faster", "Removing a conditional branch always reduces its computation time", "An eager branchless form can evaluate both candidate expressions and add selection work. Any recovery saving must be compared with those costs under matching workload and timing assumptions.", "branchless-selection"),
    ("amdahl-and-gustafson-same-fraction", "The serial fractions in Amdahl and Gustafson formulas are interchangeable", "The fixed-work fraction uses a one-worker baseline; Gustafson's fraction uses the scaled parallel run. Preserve the different denominators and workload comparisons, or convert the fractions for the same problem before comparing formulas.", "scaled-fraction"),
    ("roofline-is-achieved-rate", "A program must achieve the smaller Roofline limit", "The 2008 model supplies an upper envelope from matched compute, bandwidth and DRAM-boundary intensity assumptions. Dependencies, insufficient parallelism and other limits can keep the program below that envelope.", "roofline-ceiling"),
    ("cpu-profile-proves-bottleneck", "The largest CPU profile entry proves which change will help the workload most", "A profile measures its selected event population and attribution rule. It can motivate a workload-specific candidate, but a controlled comparison must test an improvement; collecting a profile does not remove sampling bias or observation overhead.", "bottleneck-hypothesis"),
    ("cpu-time-equals-response-time", "Aggregate CPU time is the same as end to end request latency", "Parallel threads can accumulate more CPU time than elapsed time, while off-CPU critical-path waits increase latency without running that thread. Population, timing boundary and overlap distinguish the metrics.", "cpu-time"),
    ("allocation-rate-means-per-op", "A lower allocation byte rate proves that each operation allocates less", "Bytes per second combines actual bytes per completed operation with throughput over matching boundaries. Lower throughput can lower the rate without lowering per-operation allocation; neither quantity alone measures retained heap growth.", "allocation-rate"),
    ("syscall-always-switches-task", "Every system call switches to another scheduled thread", "A trap enters the kernel, but a nonblocking service can return to the same task. A scheduled context switch is a separate event depending on runnable state and scheduler policy.", "syscall-entry"),
    ("warmup-proves-stability", "Completing configured warmup proves the later measurement is stationary", "Warmup runs a selected workload and can train specific state. It does not prove that JIT activity, contention, frequency or other relevant conditions remain stable throughout later observations.", "stationarity"),
    ("fork-resets-hardware", "A fresh JMH fork guarantees empty hardware caches and an untrained predictor", "Forking separates process-local JVM histories. It does not promise to reset shared hardware or external activity; cold cache and predictor conditions need their own setup or explicit model assumptions.", "fork-isolation"),
    ("consumption-preserves-instructions", "Returning a benchmark result preserves every instruction used to compute it", "Result consumption makes the required value matter, but folding, hoisting or another equivalent implementation may still compute it. Establish the benchmark's actual work instead of inferring executed instructions from source expressions alone.", "result-use"),
    ("repetition-erases-bias", "Enough timing repetitions eliminate drift and every systematic error", "Repetition can characterize sample variation, but drift, quantization and workload mismatch need not cancel. The balanced-order model addresses only its specified time pattern, not every bias.", "sample-variance"),
    ("reuse-guarantees-residency", "A repeated access to the same data guarantees a cache hit", "Residency also depends on intervening lines, capacity, mapping and replacement or invalidation. Enough distinct intervening demands evict a line in the declared fully associative LRU model even though the program reuses it later.", "temporal-reuse"),
    ("throughput-is-reciprocal-latency", "Request latency is always one divided by throughput", "That relation holds for the stated closed serial sequence with no idle or overlap and its matching mean latency. Concurrent requests, queueing, tail quantiles and different boundaries generally break the simple reciprocal relation.", "throughput"),
    ("allocation-always-enters-kernel", "Every successful allocation requests new operating system memory", "An allocator can satisfy a request from existing managed capacity through a local free-list or bump path. A backing refill is a separate operation determined by the allocator policy and available resources.", "allocator-fastpath"),
]
BOUNDARIES = [
    ("metric-population-and-boundary", "Metrics need matching units and populations", "Completed requests, elapsed time, summed CPU time, mean response latency and a latency quantile measure different things. The reciprocal throughput identity requires its closed serial no-idle population. A timing comparison must complete the same verified workload and count the same logical unit; moving setup outside the clock boundary does not make that setup faster.", "measurement-boundary"),
    ("fixed-versus-scaled-work", "Parallel laws use different serial denominators", "The Amdahl equations here are an explicit ideal fixed-work decomposition, not quotations of historical empirical fractions from 1967. Gustafson normalizes the scaled parallel run instead. Extra overhead, load imbalance, indivisible work or changed per-work cache costs can invalidate an ideal equality; superlinear measured results can reflect changed model assumptions rather than refuting the algebra.", "amdahl-fraction"),
    ("roofline-boundary-and-ceiling", "A Roofline is a scoped ceiling model", "The 2008 Berkeley profile uses operations per DRAM byte after cache filtering and a matching sustainable bandwidth estimate. Count precision, operation convention, reads/writes and bytes consistently. The minimum of the compute and bandwidth roofs is potential performance, not guaranteed throughput, and its memory-bound classification does not prove that a short dependent-load workload saturates memory bandwidth.", "roofline-ceiling"),
    ("cache-policy-and-observation", "Cache conclusions require their mapping and replacement model", "Geometry and reuse-distance rules name the abstract address, allocation and LRU policies. The equal-capacity fully associative reference is a classification model, not an optimal-replacement oracle. Increasing fully associative LRU capacity has a stack property; changing associativity and set mapping at fixed capacity is different. Coherence, prefetch, bypass and competing accesses require their own treatment.", "associativity"),
    ("local-miss-denominators", "Local miss rates are conditional observations", "Level-two local misses are divided by accesses that reach level two, not every original demand. If no demand reaches that level, its observed local miss fraction is undefined rather than automatically zero. The DRAM-byte formula assumes its per-miss allocation/transfer policy and excludes uncounted writes, prefetch, coalescing and lower cache levels.", "l2-miss-rate"),
    ("predictor-model-boundary", "Two bit state is a declared predictor model", "The counter, threshold, initial state, update timing and tagless index function are stated model inputs, not a reconstruction of a modern Intel predictor. Bias alone does not determine mismatch rate. Branch removal suppresses the eliminated site's predictions but can introduce other work; no fixed recovery penalty or universal branchless speedup is inferred.", "counter-state"),
    ("vector-and-memory-overlap", "Grouping and overlap do not eliminate every cost", "Vector grouping and scalar tails preserve the declared useful work while changing instruction execution. Supported operations, legality, setup, dependencies and resource limits remain relevant. The immediate-start fluid-transfer equation excludes startup/drain delays; merely adding requests does not erase finite-batch latency. Serial cost sums and fully overlapped resource bounds are distinct models.", "simd-execution"),
    ("allocation-and-kernel-boundary", "Allocation bookkeeping is not every memory cost", "A fast allocation path may avoid an allocation syscall while initialization or later first-touch faults still cost work. Actual bytes per operation differ from bytes per second and retained heap growth. Headroom and collection triggers are explicit policies, and the fluid budget is an approximation. Kernel entry, a page fault and a scheduled thread switch are distinct events, with architecture-dependent restoration costs.", "allocator-fastpath"),
    ("profile-attribution-boundary", "Profiles are measurements of their selected event population", "Uniform CPU-time sampling is an explicit ideal model; periodic or hardware-event samples need not represent wall time in the same way and may lose precision through short events, unwinding or skid. Inclusive frame counts overlap, while the declared self counts belong to leaves. An estimated CPU hotspot is an unconfirmed optimization candidate, especially when off-CPU waits dominate the response path.", "profile"),
    ("harness-lifecycle-boundary", "Warmup and forks manage limited state", "JMH 1.37 warmup and measurement are configured phases whose invocations must complete normally for the stated results. Warmup does not certify stationarity. A new JVM excludes earlier process-local feedback only under the no-import assumption; it still creates its own history and shares host hardware and load. Measured phase costs must not be presented as full startup cost unless that is the chosen boundary.", "harness"),
    ("consumption-and-operation-credit", "A required result does not preserve an instruction recipe", "The JMH result/sink contract blocks the specified unused-result deletion, not every legal optimization. Constant folding and invariant-work hoisting can survive consumption. Operations-per-invocation is a reporting declaration that must match the real logical workload; it is not proof that a source loop performed that many independent meaningful operations or materialized allocations.", "result-use"),
    ("noise-quantization-and-drift", "Statistical claims need their stated noise model", "The strict plus/minus-one-tick elapsed-error bound uses the declared floor quantizer, not an arbitrary physical clock. The n-minus-one variance expectation requires independent identically distributed finite-variance samples, not stationarity alone. ABBA balances mean time position in the exact shared linear-drift model; it neither removes nonlinear/interacting drift nor automatically recovers the time-zero speedup ratio.", "balanced-order"),
]
EXAMPLES = [
    ("parallel-cpu-time-and-latency", "Four simultaneous requests do not have reciprocal throughput latency", "In the declared zero-overhead model, four independent requests each require one second on one CPU slot and all start together on four slots. They complete after one second: aggregate CPU time is four seconds, throughput is four operations/s and every response latency is one second, not 1/4 second. Running the same requests serially with immediate next arrivals takes four wall seconds and gives one operation/s with one-second mean latency.", ["cpu-time", "wall-time", "latency", "throughput", "concurrency"]),
    ("same-problem-parallel-fraction-conversion", "Amdahl and Gustafson agree after converting denominators", "For an ideal fixed workload with one-worker time normalized to one, serial fraction 1/10 and four equal workers give parallel time 1/10+(9/10)/4=13/40 and speedup 40/13. In that SAME parallel run the serial fraction is (1/10)/(13/40)=4/13. Gustafson's expression then gives 4-3*(4/13)=40/13 too. Substituting the original 1/10 unchanged into the scaled-run formula would describe different assumptions.", ["amdahl-fraction", "parallel-time", "speedup", "scaled-fraction", "gustafson-speedup"]),
    ("equal-capacity-associativity-counterexample", "A direct mapped cache can protect a useful line from unrelated eviction", "In two declared cold two-line read-allocating caches, process block IDs [0,1,3,0]. Direct mapping by block modulo 2 sends 1 and 3 to the same set while 0 stays in the other, giving three misses and a final hit. Fully associative LRU evicts 0 on access 3 and misses again on the final 0, giving four misses. With common hit cost 1 and incremental miss penalty 4, mean costs are 4 and 5 abstract time units. The three first-seen blocks are compulsory misses; the last fully associative miss is noncompulsory capacity loss.", ["associativity", "set-index", "compulsory-miss", "capacity-miss", "mean-access-cost"]),
    ("conditional-cache-rate-accounting", "Local downstream misses must be multiplied by upstream traffic", "A declared blocking two-level read hierarchy sees 100 original demands, 10 level-one misses and 2 level-two misses. Thus m1=1/10, local m2=1/5 and the global memory fraction is 1/50. Incremental costs h1=1,h2=4,D=50 give mean cost 1+(1/10)*(4+(1/5)*50)=12/5. If each downstream miss independently fetches an uncoalesced 16-byte line from DRAM and no other traffic is counted, DRAM traffic is 32 bytes, not 100*(1/5)*16.", ["l1-miss-rate", "l2-miss-rate", "mean-access-cost", "memory-traffic"]),
    ("packed-and-strided-cold-reads", "Packing useful elements can reduce DRAM transfers in the one cache model", "The declared single cold cache has 16-byte lines, at least one line of capacity, demand allocation on every read miss and direct DRAM backing, with no prefetch or other traffic. Eight useful 4-byte elements at addresses 0,4,...,28 occupy two lines and require 32 transferred bytes. The same eight logical values laid out at addresses 0,16,...,112 occupy eight lines and require 128 bytes. This comparison changes layout under the explicit model, not the useful operation count.", ["spatial-locality", "line-granularity", "working-set", "cold-cache", "memory-traffic"]),
    ("reuse-distance-across-capacity-threshold", "Repeated data still misses when the reuse distance reaches capacity", "For the declared read-only trace [0,1,2,0,1,2], a cold fully associative two-line LRU cache misses all six times. Each repeated line has two distinct intervening lines, enough to evict it; there are four evictions in total. A three-line cache with the same allocation/LRU rules has only the first three compulsory misses and no evictions. The claim relies on this fixed LRU capacity expansion, not arbitrary changes in hardware associativity or prefetch.", ["reuse-distance", "cache-capacity", "eviction", "capacity-miss", "temporal-reuse"]),
    ("alternation-and-predictor-seed", "The same alternating outcomes can have different steady mismatch patterns", "The declared counter predicts taken at state>=2, updates after the prediction and saturates within 0..3. Trace [taken,not-taken] repeated four times produces four mismatches from initial state 0: states alternate 1,0. From initial state 1 it produces eight mismatches: states alternate 2,1. Outcome frequency alone therefore cannot supply the mismatch rate without the initialization and update policy.", ["cold-predictor", "counter-state", "branch-outcomes", "counter-update", "misprediction"]),
    ("opposed-branches-sharing-one-counter", "Predictor index aliasing couples two otherwise simple streams", "In the declared tagless model, branch A is always taken and B always not taken, with execution A,B repeated four times. Separate counters starting at 1 incur only A's first mismatch; B immediately predicts not taken correctly. If both sites map to one counter initially 1, A increments it to 2 after a mismatch and B decrements it to 1 after another mismatch, yielding eight. For the stated index floor(pc/4) modulo 2, PCs 0 and 8 demonstrate such a collision without describing a real Intel predictor hash.", ["branch-site", "predictor-alias", "branch-outcomes", "counter-update", "misprediction"]),
    ("roofline-resource-units", "Improving memory traffic can reach but not exceed a compute roof", "In an abstract 2008-style Roofline calculation, W=64 FLOPs and Q=32 DRAM bytes give intensity 2 FLOPs/byte. With compatible compute ceiling 16 FLOPs/time-unit and sustainable bandwidth ceiling 4 bytes/time-unit, the roof is min(16,8)=8 FLOPs/time-unit and the elapsed lower bound is 64/8=8 units. Reducing traffic to 16 bytes raises the roof to 16 and lowers that bound to 4. Actual execution can still be slower; source load bytes before caching are not Q.", ["memory-traffic", "arithmetic-intensity", "memory-bandwidth", "compute-ceiling", "roofline-ceiling"]),
    ("simd-tail-and-memory-limit", "Four lanes need not deliver four times lower elapsed time", "A declared loop has ten independent scalar operations and width four, so it executes two full vector groups and two scalar tail operations. If each vector or scalar operation costs one unit with no setup, control or overlap, modeled arithmetic service falls from 10 to 4 units, a 2.5-fold improvement. In a separate ideal whole-kernel model that fully overlaps arithmetic with an unchanged 8-unit memory transfer, completion changes only from max(10,8)=10 to max(4,8)=8 units.", ["vector-width", "vector-groups", "tail-work", "simd-execution", "service-time"]),
    ("batch-entries-and-task-switches", "Fewer kernel entries and fewer task switches are different claims", "For ten requests and successful batch capacity four, the declared policy issues three calls instead of ten. With entry overhead 2 and per-item cost 1, kernel service work is 3*2+10=16 rather than 10*2+10=30 units, excluding waits and other costs. If each service returns to the same task with no scheduling event, both traces can have zero thread switches. Separately, a blocking call whose wait remains unresolved can select another runnable thread; kernel entry alone did not force that choice.", ["batch-size", "entry-count", "kernel-work", "syscall-entry", "context-switch"]),
    ("allocation-metrics-and-headroom", "Lower bytes per operation can coexist with higher allocation pressure", "In the declared matching intervals, 100 materialized bytes/op at 1000 operations/s is 100000 bytes/s, whereas 50 bytes/op at 3000 operations/s is 150000 bytes/s. Per-operation allocation fell but the rate rose. A separate fluid budget with 600000 bytes of headroom and no reclamation would reach zero after 6 or 4 seconds at those constant rates. These are not universal GC periods; an individual fitting request can still use existing allocator capacity without a new OS memory request.", ["allocation-per-op", "allocation-rate", "heap-headroom", "collection-work", "allocator-fastpath"]),
    ("inclusive-self-and-offcpu-attribution", "Inclusive counts overlap and CPU samples omit off CPU delay", "For three unit-weight stacks [main,a,a], [main,a,b], [main,b], the declared presence-once inclusive rule gives main=3,a=2,b=2, while leaf self counts are main=0,a=1,b=2. Summing inclusive counts does not recover three units of disjoint time. Region b is the largest observed self entry, but independent tracing of a request with 10 ms CPU work and 100 ms unoverlapped off-CPU wait would still leave 100 ms if all CPU work disappeared in that fixed-cost comparison.", ["profile", "inclusive-cost", "self-cost", "hotspot", "offcpu-wait"]),
    ("jmh-phase-and-constant-result", "A correctly consumed benchmark result can still be constant folded", "A declared JMH 1.37 run starts a fresh JVM fork, performs five configured warmup iterations there and then records its measurement iterations. A benchmark returning the pure constant expression (3*5)+(7*2) may return 29 after legal folding even though the harness consumes the result; that does not measure two runtime multiplies and an add. Five iterations are not five invocations or proof of stationarity, and the fresh process does not certify cold host caches. Operations-per-invocation must match the real logical work rather than repair that inference by changing a denominator.", ["harness", "warmup", "fork-isolation", "result-use", "folded-benchmark"]),
    ("quantization-and-balanced-drift", "Repeated timing cannot remove every systematic effect", "For the declared floor timer with delta=1 unit, true interval [0.9,1.1] lasts 0.2 but measures 1, error +0.8; [0.1,1.9] lasts 1.8 but also measures 1, error -0.8. In a separate exact noiseless same-work trial model, costs are mu_A=10,mu_B=20 plus shared drift 2*t at positions 0,1,2,3. ABBA observes A=10,B=22,B=24,A=16, giving means 13 and 23 and their common-position ratio 13/23, not the time-zero ratio 1/2. Nonlinear drift and noise need additional treatment.", ["timer-resolution", "timer-error", "balanced-order", "timing-samples", "speedup"]),
]
